21 1080 "I only" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501321 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501321 1083 #uuid "4566a58e-dd27-57f2-8c17-67e13d84f401" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501317 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501321 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501326 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501328 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501331 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1077 "selection" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501331 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501322 1079 #uuid "5912bad9-fd12-5d7e-8bfc-20dae374c49f" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501332 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501323 1071 "![](images/76/7688f7536a66b7a737d58031b9b8752aabaa84a914ff5deb799e7060d7a90aeb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501323 1072 #uuid "61c31858-4aad-578b-a21e-e968c5ca8b40" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501323 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(6,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/cb/cbec3e69d5f8a5bbcfc7d97d95c9f3d3ec93be63b1c8de2ceba3e3a24a9dba13.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+\\frac{2}{3}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(6,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(6)+4 \\\\ 0 & =-4+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(6,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{2}{3}(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{2}{3}(6)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4+4 \\\\ 0 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501324 1080 "III only" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501324 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501324 1083 #uuid "7d397adc-2e01-5cd8-a945-f4855a2fd4b8" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501325 1080 "III only" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501325 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501325 1083 #uuid "92cee00b-fc75-54a9-b753-d48fe2943e17" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501326 1080 "III only" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501326 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [17592186501326 1083 #uuid "9a9607e4-f2bb-5686-8bd2-b386551546bd" 13194139535373 17592186045443 true] [175